WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:05.080
I think Trump, in lots of ways, is a refreshing
reminder

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:08.920
that the guy that's in the White House is
another human being.

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:15.119
And that bringing that power back to human
scale is an important part

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:22.360
of what needs to be done to correct the insanity
that's been going on in the post-war era,

00:00:25.869 --> 00:00:28.460
Louis Rossetto is the co-founder of Wired
Magazine

00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:33.380
and the co-recipient of the very first Lanny
Friedlander Prize from Reason.

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.300
It honors an individual or group who has created
a publication,

00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:40.940
medium, or distribution platform that vastly
expands human freedom.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.710
In 1971, while an undergraduate at Columbia
University,

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.720
he co-authored a story in the New York Times
Magazine titled,

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:50.980
"The New Right Credo: Libertarianism".

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:57.160
"Liberalism, conservatism, and leftist radicalism
are all bankrupt philosophies," he wrote.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:01.820
"Refugees from the old right, the old left,
and the new left are organizing independently

00:01:01.820 --> 00:01:04.780
under the banner of libertarianism."

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:07.420
Rossetto's newest project is a novel

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:11.800
called, "Change is Good: A Story from the
Heroic Era of the Internet,"

00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:15.540
which he and designer Erik Spiekermann launched
on Kickstarter.

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:20.420
The project raised nearly 200,000$ and is
going out to backers now.

00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:21.960
Louis, thanks for talking.

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:22.860
Pleasure to be here.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.840
What's the plot of, Change is Good?

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.100
It's a novel right?

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:29.360
That's an easy question to start with.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:33.900
There are six characters, each of them have
their own stories.

00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.020
I suppose for you, at this moment,

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:45.060
the most interesting thing about the book
is the focus on that time.

00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:48.979
The book is set in the 1990's.

00:01:48.980 --> 00:01:54.240
The 1990's were this pivotal moment in world
history, where ...

00:01:55.740 --> 00:01:56.860
Well, cast your mind back.

00:01:56.869 --> 00:02:00.560
It was a period of unbelievable optimism.

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.300
The Cold War is over.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:03.700
The internet is taking off.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.289
It's becoming a mass medium.

00:02:05.289 --> 00:02:06.380
The Cold War end-

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:07.740
The economy is doing great.

00:02:07.750 --> 00:02:11.181
The Cold War ends at the beginning of the
90's.

00:02:11.181 --> 00:02:19.540
The internet takes off with the arrival of
the web.

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:23.970
The period ... There's drugs: ecstasy.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:26.360
The rave culture.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:33.680
Multiple different levels there are massive
changes going on that are affecting all aspects

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:36.050
of our life.

00:02:36.050 --> 00:02:42.530
The 90's is this pivotal moment where there's
this burst of optimism.

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:48.740
Young people are arriving in this new space,
the internet, the web,

00:02:48.740 --> 00:02:52.020
digital technologies in general.

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:55.480
There's a sense that the future is utterly
malleable.

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.580
For the first time in a long time, after the
Cold War,

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:04.440
the fear and anxiety of the Cold War, now
there's this open running room.

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:06.040
It's the end of history, literally.

00:03:06.040 --> 00:03:08.880
Fukuyama says so.

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.020
Now, anything's possible.

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:18.040
Any weird, crazy idea that you have, is no
longer bound by normal restrictions,

00:03:18.050 --> 00:03:22.180
like finance, or law, or even physical bounds.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.930
It is incredibly hard to go back to those
early days of even pre-internet culture.

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:33.200
Even AOL, American Online, had the walled-garden,
where people were anonymous.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:37.600
You had fake names that you would use and
talk about anything.

00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:39.060
Usenet groups.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:41.180
You could reproduce things cheaply.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.800
DVD players were 7$, or whatever.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:49.180
I mean, companies that had zero income had
billion dollar valuations.

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:52.740
Companies were more valuable than General
Motors

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.780
that were selling in the tens of millions
of dollars.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:04:00.750
It had Bill Clinton saying the era of big
government is over.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:05.340
There were all sorts of things happening in
society, at that moment,

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:07.580
in all levels of society,

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:14.200
that were pointing toward a millennial future
that was going to be almost utopian.

00:04:14.200 --> 00:04:18.540
There were people involved, in the 90's, that
were making that happen.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.400
The gen-X'ers of that time were adventurers
and revolutionaries,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:30.060
consciously looking to travel to a new land,
west of California,

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:33.710
and bring back fresh kill for the human race.

00:04:33.710 --> 00:04:35.790
That was a future.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.270
They were actively out there making that new
future.

00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:45.290
That's what's heroic, in the sense that people
felt like they could do heroic undertakings.

00:04:45.290 --> 00:04:47.461
You could do whatever ... If you could dream
it, you could do it.

00:04:47.461 --> 00:04:50.740
It was kind of like Walt Disney on ecstasy.

00:04:50.740 --> 00:04:57.720
It's heroic when you do it in the face of
danger and opposition,

00:04:57.730 --> 00:04:59.670
which you also had at the same time.

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:07.840
You were moving like Louis and Clark, into
this new territory, that was undefined.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.480
That was unmapped.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:12.620
That was full of danger in lots of ways.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.760
Any company could go under at any moment.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.780
Any weirdness could be popping out of the
things that you're discovering.

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:25.800
"Change is Good" is about six characters exploring
this new realm, like Louis and Clark,

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:32.120
going out into this uncharted territory and hoping
to discover what that was all about.

00:05:32.120 --> 00:05:35.140
In fact, also helping to create our future.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:41.260
It takes place in 1998, which is another pivotal
moment within a pivotal moment, because,

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:51.020
for most of the 90's, there was this expansionary
sense of the future looking incredibly wonderful.

00:05:51.020 --> 00:05:58.740
Then there was this ton of money that flowed
into the sector in 1998.

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:02.780
What was the digital revolution, became the
.com bubble.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.680
What was utopian became greed.

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:14.190
I'm trying to examine that particular tipping
point, between the moment when people had

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:23.150
fire in their eyes and wanted to change the
world, and they ended up being perverted,

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:31.600
in a kind of way, into, or seduced into, pursuing
the pure joy of making a lot of money.

00:06:31.600 --> 00:06:36.080
I hope one day to experience that
myself.

00:06:36.080 --> 00:06:41.960
If that was heroic age of the internet, how
would you characterize the internet now?

00:06:41.960 --> 00:06:45.120
Or America now?

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:48.140
The internet does not seem to be particularly
heroic.

00:06:48.140 --> 00:06:49.380
Does it?

00:06:49.380 --> 00:06:51.340
I think the Internet's just become
part of our life.

00:06:51.340 --> 00:06:55.480
It's gone from occult to the culture.

00:06:55.480 --> 00:07:01.560
It's just everything that's normal today is
connected, as we said it would be,

00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:07.060
to digital technologies and the networks themselves.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:10.730
I think you have to look to other places to
see where this edge is today.

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:14.370
In many ways, what James is doing, is looking
for that edge again.

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:15.370
That Neolife?

00:07:15.370 --> 00:07:16.360
That Neolife, exactly.

00:07:16.420 --> 00:07:21.900
A lot of it is ... It's kind of interesting,
'cause baby boomers obviously also took charge

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:22.910
in the 90's.

00:07:22.910 --> 00:07:27.520
I remember when Bill Clinton was the first
baby boomer president in '92.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:31.360
Baby boomers have long been accused of being
solipsistic and inward-looking, but-

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:32.560
Totally true.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.220
Also, now, literally, that's happening. Right?

00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:39.460
'Cause we're looking at our own bodies and
changing them in ways that were unimaginable,

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:40.330
even a few years ago.

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:44.800
There's a new generation that's
pursuing this new adventure.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:46.380
There are new adventurers out there.

00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:50.900
I think, the Internet's become the background.

00:07:50.900 --> 00:07:53.000
There's an edge.

00:07:53.000 --> 00:07:56.150
There are still edges all over the place.

00:07:56.150 --> 00:07:59.370
The genetic revolution is one of them.

00:07:59.370 --> 00:08:01.620
Space is another.

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.140
Transportation is another one.

00:08:04.140 --> 00:08:06.950
There are explorations to be had all over
the place.

00:08:06.950 --> 00:08:13.610
The kind of incredible fervor that you saw
with the arrival of the internet,

00:08:13.610 --> 00:08:17.800
that affected everything simultaneously all over the planet,

00:08:17.800 --> 00:08:24.560
that kind of change is unlikely to happen in the same broad way that it did then.

00:08:24.560 --> 00:08:31.500
Instead you have a bunch of little, or smaller,
changes that are erupting and that will become

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:32.420
their own major thing.

00:08:32.420 --> 00:08:34.720
There are just lots of them happening out
there.

00:08:34.720 --> 00:08:38.560
You also talk about the book, and
this brings us to Erik Spiekermann,

00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.640
but it's a revolution in printing.

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:43.300
What is unique about the printing process?

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:44.860
Why the attention to that detail?

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:52.400
Why make a bunch of atoms, put together
a bunch of atoms, to experience the story,

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:56.000
instead of just putting it out digitally?

00:08:56.000 --> 00:08:57.960
It's a very good question.

00:08:57.960 --> 00:08:59.590
Because-

00:08:59.590 --> 00:09:02.860
In the end, we also wanted to create another
experience.

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:06.020
There's a story, which is an abstraction in
lots of ways,

00:09:06.020 --> 00:09:09.960
and then, there's a physical object of the book.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:17.190
In lots of ways, books have become devalued
as they've migrated into the digital domain.

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:22.840
The book itself, as an object, has become
degraded.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:29.800
It's a mass object that is not special, that
doesn't deliver a great experience.

00:09:29.800 --> 00:09:31.070
The inks are not great.

00:09:31.070 --> 00:09:32.240
The paper's not great.

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:35.029
The binding's not great.

00:09:35.029 --> 00:09:36.920
Nothing about it is special.

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:43.150
We said, why don't we make a real ... Nevertheless,
it's still the best way to read things.

00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:50.040
We like to read things on screen, but still
paper offers the best way to read.

00:09:50.040 --> 00:09:53.840
We said, why don't we make a special experience.

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:59.880
The best experience we can make in the twenty-first
century, for reading itself.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:06.720
That required Erik to begin an exploration
of printing itself.

00:10:06.720 --> 00:10:11.400
He rediscovered an old way of printing, letterpress.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:16.940
Instead of, the more normal way everything
is printed today, which is offset.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:27.250
Letterpress is actually an image pressed into
a piece of paper.

00:10:27.250 --> 00:10:34.089
It's something that got lost in mass-marketing,
because it just couldn't make the transition

00:10:34.089 --> 00:10:40.500
to the new technologies for producing print.

00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:46.840
What Erik has done, is reinvented a way of
producing plates for letterpress,

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:52.640
which enable you to use all the digital tools for publication,

00:10:52.640 --> 00:10:58.620
but apply them to this classic technology of letterpress.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:04.580
He created tools to make the plates that go
on the press.

00:11:04.580 --> 00:11:10.430
This particular book, "Change is Good," is
among the very first to use his technology.

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:16.460
It's the first one that he's designing and
publishing himself.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:24.149
It's just a special experience to read a crisp
type, impressed in the paper.

00:11:24.149 --> 00:11:29.860
You actually have a physical impression in
the paper, which adds a third dimension

00:11:29.860 --> 00:11:31.580
to the experience of reading.

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:35.460
We don't really know about anymore, because
everything we get is offset,

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:37.680
which is completely flat.

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:44.180
You have the experience of the impression
of the type on the paper,

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:47.500
and then you also have black ink, which you never get with offset.

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:50.980
Offset is, by definition, a variance of gray.

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:55.980
This ink that's pressed into the paper is
actually black.

00:11:55.980 --> 00:12:02.960
Altogether the experience of reading, "Change
is Good," is basically state of the art

00:12:02.960 --> 00:12:04.260
for the twenty-first century.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:08.780
You also have thrown in a Spotify
soundtrack.

00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:10.280
Also, because we can.

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:11.280
Yes.

00:12:11.400 --> 00:12:17.540
If you look at the very beginning
of the book, there's a link to Spotify,

00:12:17.540 --> 00:12:22.360
which enables you to listen to the soundtrack that
we put together, which is like 10 hours of music,

00:12:22.370 --> 00:12:26.690
for listening to the book at your leisure.

00:12:26.690 --> 00:12:32.160
We've known each other for about
20 years now, or coming up on 20 years.

00:12:32.160 --> 00:12:37.600
One of the things over the past couple years
that ex- You have always been a maverick,

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:46.080
at a time when during Wired you seen as suspiciously
libertarian in your proclivities, and whatnot.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:53.040
You have surprise a lot of people over the
past couple years by being, if not pro-Trump,

00:12:53.040 --> 00:12:59.400
pro-Donald Trump, you are enjoying his intervention
into politics, into the political status quo.

00:12:59.400 --> 00:13:02.860
How do you feel about him after a year in
office?

00:13:02.870 --> 00:13:07.290
What is it about him that peaked your curiosity?

00:13:07.290 --> 00:13:11.900
What is he bringing to the stage that you
think is really vital?

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:25.360
I think for most of my life, my
tendency has been to try to diminish the power

00:13:25.360 --> 00:13:30.020
of the State, in general.

00:13:30.020 --> 00:13:36.060
Part of that is literal power, and part of
it is the power that's in your head.

00:13:36.060 --> 00:13:42.660
The presidency has taken on this otherworldly
position in people's minds

00:13:42.660 --> 00:13:49.120
that is beyond Pope-hood.

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:59.620
It's this figure of immense authority that
you obliged to respect and that has all this-

00:13:59.620 --> 00:14:02.380
and has the-

00:14:02.380 --> 00:14:05.720
Because of the power of the United States
in general, has the ability

00:14:05.720 --> 00:14:09.680
to project that power all over the planet.

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:17.700
I think Trump, in lots of ways, is a refreshing
reminder that the guy that's in the White House

00:14:17.700 --> 00:14:20.460
is another human being.

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:24.440
The power of the State is way too exalted.

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:33.600
Bringing that power back to human scale is
an important part of what needs to be done

00:14:33.600 --> 00:14:39.040
to correct the insanity that's been going
on in the post-war era,

00:14:39.040 --> 00:14:45.160
where you have these large institutions that control all aspects of our lives.

00:14:45.160 --> 00:14:52.740
Leaching respect and authority out of the
State, is kind of a good thing, on its own level.

00:14:54.900 --> 00:14:56.640
Maybe I'll just leave it at that.

00:14:56.649 --> 00:14:58.660
Well we will leave it there.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:03.850
We've been talking with Louis Rossetto, the
co-founder of Wired and the novelist behind,

00:15:03.850 --> 00:15:10.760
"Change is Good: A Story of the Heroic Era
of the Internet" and a revolution in printing.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:12.640
Louis, thanks for talking.

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:13.640
Pleasure, as always.

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:15.640
For Reason, I'm Nick Gillespie.

